\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS - Ringgit falls below 2.53/dlr on rumours, technicals. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Madhav Reddy \par \uc2 586 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Oktober 1996\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1996 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  KUALA LUMPUR, Oct 30 (Reuter) - U.S. funds and speculators combined rumours and technical reasons to pull down the ringgit sharply to below 2.53 per dollar, dealers said on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  By 1130 GMT, the ringgit had dipped to 2.5305/15 a dollar in London after closing at 2.5250/60 in Kuala Lumpur and against 2.5135/40 at close of trading on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  It has lost over 275 points from around 2.5025 on Friday. \par \par \uc2  The dealers said some large speculators based in Singapore and London were testing the tolerance levels of Bank Negara \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which usually \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to smooth sharp fluctuations in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "They are testing how long Bank Negara will tolerate this sell down and they are using rumours to do that," said a dealer with a U.S. bank in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  "At least $300 to $400 (million) was bought today," the dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said it was widely rumoured that Bank Negara was comfortable with the decline of the ringgit to between 2.52 and 2.53 per dollar. \par \par \uc2  Bank Negara did not immediately have a comment. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the market also used an overnight report from London that the U.S. was seeking sanctions against Malaysia's national oil firm Petronas for its involvement in an Iranian oil project to push the ringgit lower. \par \par \uc2  The report was used despite U.S. Energy Secretary Hazel O'Leary's comment later that the Clinton government would wait until after the November 5 presidential election to decide how it will enforce sanctions against Libya and Iran. \par \par \uc2  "The ringgit continues to be susceptible to rumours in the market as players search for excuses to sell the ringgit," research firm I.D.E.A. said in a comment late on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  It said there was also talk about Bank Negara buying U.S. dollars at around 2.5180 per dollar level. \par \par \uc2  "Neither (of these reasons) look credible," I.D.E.A. said. \par \par \uc2  It said a firm ringgit would help keep imported inflation low as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  maintains a firm monetary policy and reduce value of imports, particularly against the Japanese yen, when the country is trying to cut its negative balance of payments. However, dealers predicted that the ringgit was likely to go down further unless there was profit-taking or a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  They said year-end requirement of dollars by Malaysian corporate sector, repatriation of profits by foreign investors, and government's forecast of large monthly trade deficits for the rest of the year in the Economic Report for 1996/97 were expected to pull down the ringgit. \par \par \uc2  "Once the 2.5180 per dollar resistance was broken, the market was more willing to hold dollars than before," said a senior dealer with a Malaysian bank in Kuala Lumpur. \par \par \uc2  He said beyond 2.5180, there was also a lot of unwinding of long ringgit positions against the Singapore dollar. "Even structured (portfolio) positions are being unwound," he said. \par \par \uc2  Another Malaysian \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer said it was possible that Bank Negara was having problems keeping down domestic interbank liquidity, which has several times threatened its tight monetary policy and forced it to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  However, the \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the open market to mop up liquidity was becoming expensive, so Bank Negara may be allowing some of the short-term offshore funds to flow out, he said. \par \par \uc2  - Kuala Lumpur newsroom (603-230 8911) \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1996 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011018dsau01624\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
