\par\par \b \uc2  Malaysia Ringgit -2: Market Believed Too Regulated \b0\par\par\uc2 208 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 August 1996\par 11:12\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1996, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  A ringgit dealer from a local bank who declined to be identified said some foreign investors were focusing on trading of the Thai baht and Indonesian rupiah in the absence of news concerning the Malaysian ringgit. \par \par \uc2  'Nobody's taking any position in the market this past week,' he said, noting that dealers had been going for early lunches and taking off early. \par \par \uc2  He also said that offshore fund managers were inactive because some 'hold the perception that the Malaysian market is very regulated.' \par \par \uc2  'They believed that if they make large buyings or selling, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may suspect that they are speculating on the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,' he said. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Bank Negara \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2 , last made an open \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Aug. 23, when it borrowed one-month funds at 7.05%. \par \par \uc2  In the money market, the one-month Kuala Lumpur Interbank Offered Rates (KLIBOR) were at 7.25%, up from 7.20% on Thursday. The three-month KLIBOR were at 7.31%, up from 7.30%. The six-month KLIBOR were unchanged at 7.35%. \par \par \uc2  The overnight rates, which indicate short-term liquidity, rose to 8.30% from 6.50% on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 30-08-96 \par \par \uc2  0912GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011013ds8u06zrs\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
