\par\par \b \uc2  Malaysia Ringgit -2: Large-Scale Buying, Selling \b0\par\par\uc2 189 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Mai 1996\par 11:18\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1996, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  A ringgit dealer in Singapore said that Tuesday morning, a Japanese trust company was selling dollars to buy ringgit 'very agressively,' strengthening the Malaysian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . At that point, the dollar was at 2.4880 ringgit - its lowest point of the day. \par \par \uc2  Then several Malaysian banks stepped in to buy dollars at that level, keeping the ringgit from strengthening further. The move fueled unconfirmed rumors that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Bank Negara \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2 , had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Several U.S. investment houses stepped in to sell dollars for ringgit. But a Hong Kong party came into the market and bought two lots of dollars with ringgit - each of $90 million - when the dollar was trading at 2.4900 ringgit. He says that pushed the dollar toward its highest point of the day - 2.4935 ringgit. \par \par \uc2  The dealer said the large-scale buying and selling 'neutralized' moves in the ringgit. \par \par \uc2  A ringgit dealer in Malaysia added that false rumors that the finance minister had resigned pushed the dollar briefly to 2.4940 ringgit from 2.4900 ringgit. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 14-05-96 \par \par \uc2  0918GMT \par \par \uc2  1026 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011013ds5e0410m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
