\par\par \b \uc2  Malaysia banking liquidity up sharply in January. \b0\par\par\uc2 384 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 April 1996\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1996 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  KUALA LUMPUR, April 17 (Reuter) - Liquidity in the Malaysian banking system expanded in January 1996 with a resource surplus of 4.5 billion ringgit against 417 million the month before, the national Bernama news agency quoted Bank Negara as saying. \par \par \uc2  Bank Negara, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said in its latest monthly statistical bulletin that the expansion was due to excess liquid assets held by the banking system. \par \par \uc2  Liquid assets rose to 2.8 billion ringgit at the end of January 1996, compared with 1.5 billion at the end of December 1995, it said. \par \par \uc2  Although liquidity expanded, interbank interest rates edged upwards in the second half of January after remaining generally stable in the first half of the month. \par \par \uc2  "\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Bank Negara \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2  to maintain stability in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market as the ringgit came under speculative pressure during the third week was contractionary on liquidity. \par \par \uc2  "As a consequence, interbank rates increased across the board to 7.02-7.20 percent on January 19, 1996, from 5.96-6.88 percent at the end of December 1995," it said. \par \par \uc2  On total loans and advances (excluding housing loans sold to national mortgage corporation, Cagamas Bhd) extended by the banking institutions, Bank Negara said they moderated to increase by 4.9 billion ringgit in January, or a two percent rise year-on-year, against an increase of 7.5 billion, or a 3.1 percent rise, in December 1995. \par \par \uc2  Bernama said Bank Negara attributed the moderation in loan growth mainly to slower increases in loans extended to the broad property and manufacturing sectors and for consumption purposes which rose by 1.3 billion ringgit, 600,000 ringgit and 900,000 ringgit, respectively. \par \par \uc2  In addition, loans extended to individuals for the purchase of stocks and shares and units of unit trust funds registered a net repayment of 500,000 ringgit, it said. \par \par \uc2  "These, however, were partially offset by higher loans channelled to the finance, insurance and business services sector (2.5 billion ringgit). \par \par \uc2  "On an annual basis, growth in total loans and advances remained relatively unchanged at 28.2 percent at the end of January for the fourth consecutive month," the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was quoted as saying by Bernama. \par \par \uc2  -- Kuala Lumpur newsroom (603-230 8911) \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1996 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011018ds4h096ew\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
