\par\par \b \uc2  Malaysian ringgit uptrend intact despite slip. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Syed Azman \par \uc2 502 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 August 1995\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  KUALA LUMPUR, Aug 1 (Reuter) - The Malaysian ringgit, under pressure against the dollar, should climb back from recent falls as the country's buoyant economy continues to lure offshore funds, analysts and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The ringgit was trading at 2.4533/43 to the dollar on Tuesday against 2.4480 a week ago. The Malaysian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit a nine-year high of 2.4380 in early June. \par \par \uc2  "We're still looking at the ringgit hitting 2.40 and probably breaching it by the end of the year," said a dealer with a foreign bank in Malaysia. \par \par \uc2  "With the economy doing very well, the uptrend for ringgit is still intact." \par \par \uc2  Malaysia's economy is heading for its eighth straight year of eight percent growth or better. \par \par \uc2  The only stumbling block towards a much stronger ringgit will be Bank Negara, \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "If not for Negara \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the ringgit should be trading at 2.40-2.42 against the dollar now," the foreign bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Bank Negara was detected in the market in late June to support the dollar and the ringgit's rise was capped at 2.44. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said that while the general perception is that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  favours a stronger ringgit to curb inflation, it may not allow it to rise too fast and risk hurting exports. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think Bank Negara will allow the ringgit to appreciate too quickly as exporters will suffer. They will let it rise gradually so as to give exporters time to adjust," said an analyst at a local brokerage. \par \par \uc2  In 1994, Malaysia exported 21 percent of its products to the U.S., its leading export market. The exports, mainly electrical and electronic goods, were worth some 32.52 billion ringgit, Bank Negara estimated. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was also holding firm due to buying against the Singapore dollar as traders capitalised on interest rate differentials between the two countries. \par \par \uc2  Bank Negara was widely seen as not letting interest rates fall much in an effort to ease inflationary pressures created by buoyant economic growth. \par \par \uc2  The Malaysian Institute of Economic Research, the country's official think tank, said recently the inflation fight is not over as pressures remain strong. The government, which began an anti-inflation drive in June aimed at curbing consumer spending, is trying to cap inflation at 4.0 percent in 1995. \par \par \uc2  Malaysia's inflation rate was 3.7 percent in 1994. \par \par \uc2  "There's no reason for rates to go up because U.S. interest rates are falling, and no reason for them to go down either given our target for inflation," said Mustafa Mohamad Nor, chief economist at Arab-Malaysian Securities. \par \par \uc2  "Unless U.S. rates are pushed higher again, I think our benchmark three-month rate will be capped at six percent this year," he said. \par \par \uc2  The three-month Kuala Lumpur Interbank Offered Rate is averaging around 5.88 percent. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011103dr81059o6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
