\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia \highlight\uc2 's Bank Negara won't  \highlight22\uc2 intervene \highlight\uc2  on ringgit. \b0\par\par\uc2 392 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Juni 1995\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  KUALA LUMPUR, June 17 (Reuter) - The Malaysian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to slow the ringgit's surge unless the country was flooded with "hot money", the national Bernama news agency quoted deputy finance minister Wong See Wah as saying. \par \par \uc2  He was also quoted as saying he hoped the country's Gross Domestic Product (GDP) would grow by 9.9 percent in the second quarter, as it did in the first quarter. \par \par \uc2  Wong said although the ringgit was strengthening against the dollar, the situation was different from last year when a heavy inflow of speculative money forced the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Bank Negara, to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "It has always been the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policy to let the ringgit dictate its own movement against major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , especially the U.S. dollar, by floating it in the open market," he was quoted as telling reporters at an accounting conference. \par \par \uc2  "Unless the hot money situation re-emerges, which is not very beneficial to our country, we will continue to let the ringgit move on its own strength," Wong was quoted as saying. \par \par \uc2  The ringgit has surged in recent weeks, fuelled by dollar weakness against the major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Malaysia's robust economy and high Malaysian interest rates. \par \par \uc2  The ringgit ended Friday trading at 2.4433/43, up about five percent against 2.5575/85 at the end of last year, and close to nine-year intraday trading high of around 2.4370/80. \par \par \uc2  Bank Negara in early 1994 introduced tough capital control measures to choke off ringgit speculation and the flood of hot money. \par \par \uc2  These controls, most of which have been lifted, include a ban on sale of short-term money market instruments and long-term papers to foreigners and a negative interest rate chargeon foreign-held ringgit accounts. \par \par \uc2  On the GDP, Wong was quoted as saying: "We hope the figure will be consistent with that of the first quarter." \par \par \uc2  "Hopefully, we will be able to maintain the same figure, as a sudden drop or a sudden growth of the economy within this year is not good for the country," Wong said. \par \par \uc2  The 9.9 percent growth in the first quarter compares against 9.0 percent in the fourth quarter of last year and 8.8 percent in the first quarter of that year. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011103dr6h06qi2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
