\par\par \b \uc2  Malaysian Ringgit Higher Vs Dlr In Range-Bound Trading \b0\par\par\uc2 200 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Juni 1995\par 11:15\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1995, Dow Jones & Co., Inc.) \par \par \uc2  KUALA LUMPUR (AP-DJ)--The Malaysian ringgit, buoyed by strong commercial demand, ended higher against the U.S. dollar Monday, traders said. \par \par \uc2  At the close, the dollar was traded lower at 2.4532 ringgit, from Friday's close of 2.4585 ringgit. Trading was quiet and confined to a fairly narrow 30-point band. \par \par \uc2  Sentiment on the ringgit was generally bullish and traders were attributing the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength to the stock market, which has been seeing strong buying support from foreign funds in the last few weeks. \par \par \uc2  'Also, the ringgit is attractive because of interest rate differentials...local rates are still high compared with Singapore rates,' the chief dealer at a large local bank said. \par \par \uc2  Last Friday, Bank Negara \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2  was rumored to have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market when the ringgit weakened to 2.4680 against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  'The market was reading that as a signal the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  doesn't want to see a ringgit above 2.4700 against the dollar,' a trader said. But the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  wasn't heard in the market Monday, he said. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 05-06-95 \par \par \uc2  0915GMT \par \par \uc2  1026 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011025dr65045l0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
