\par\par \b \uc2  Malaysia  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  boosts ringgit ahead of data. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Syed Azman \par \uc2 475 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 1995\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  KUALA LUMPUR, March 28 (Reuter) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Bank Negara has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  strongly in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, driving up the ringgit against the U.S. dollar ahead of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's annual report which is expected to show a large current account deficit, money dealers said. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which will release its 1994 report on Wednesday, sold the dollar heavily last Friday to ward off ringgit selling from offshore banks and came in aggressively again on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Bank Negara was estimated to have sold about $800 million over the two days, helping the ringgit to gain 120 points against the dollar. The ringgit strengthened further on Tuesday morning to 2.5360 to the dollar although the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stayed away from the market. \par \par \uc2  Bank Negara seems to be capping dollar gains against the ringgit but the actual reason behind the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is open for speculation, financial analysts said. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be protectively pushing up the ringgit on fears the market will over-react after the release of the current account data, they said. \par \par \uc2  If the ringgit is to weaken after the report, it would probably fall back to its earlier level of around 2.55 ringgit to the dollar due to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action. \par \par \uc2  "Negara is sending a strong signal for people not to mess around with the ringgit even if the deficit is wider. They're trying to show who's the boss," said an economist at a foreign brokerage. \par \par \uc2  Dealers also said the pressure on the ringgit could also be due to the repatriation of profits by Japanese investors before they close their books for the March year-end. \par \par \uc2  "Negara could be trying to pump in liquidity (by releasing dollars) in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to balance outward remittances which are quite huge," said a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at a local bank. \par \par \uc2  "Also, a lot of payments for importation of goods are being carried out now," he said. \par \par \uc2  Financial markets in Malaysia have been speculating that Bank Negara will report a widening of the current account deficit to between 15 and 17 billion ringgit for 1994, higher than 6.3 billion in 1993 and the official forecast of 12 billion. Offshore investors, apparently concerned about the deficit, have been dumping ringgit lately, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Some foreign fund managers have drawn parallels to Mexico, which sharply devalued its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the midst of its balance of payments crisis. \par \par \uc2  Malaysian officials have expressed annoyance with that comparison, saying its foreign reserves are strong at 79 billion ringgit and its foreign debt manageable at around 50 billion ringgit. \par \par \uc2  Deputy Prime Minister Anwar Ibrahim had said Malaysia's deficit was widening due to larger imports of capital equipment as a result of surging foreign investment. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011103dr3s096cm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
