\par\par \b \uc2  Malaysian Ringgit Higher Vs Dollar; Bank Negara  \highlight22\uc2 Intervenes \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 218 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 1995\par 10:53\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1995, Dow Jones & Co., Inc.) \par \par \uc2  KUALA LUMPUR (AP-DJ)--The Malaysian ringgit was sharply higher against the U.S. dollar Friday after Bank Negara \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  during the last half hour of trading, traders said. \par \par \uc2  At the close, the dollar was lower at 2.5450 ringgit, from Thursday's 2.5495-ringgit. \par \par \uc2  A trader with a large local bank said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  came in when the ringgit fell to around 2.5525 ringgit against the dollar, pressured by offshore selling. Bank Negara sold dollars for ringgit to boost the ringgit to 2.5450 ringgit against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , he said. \par \par \uc2  'We believe the amount sold by Bank Negara was quite substantial to drive the ringgit up by more than 50 points,' he said, adding that many players were caught by surprise. \par \par \uc2  The market saw jittery offshore players unloading the ringgit, as they were worried that Bank Negara Malaysia could announce a larger-than-expected current account deficit next week, when it releases its 1994 annual report, traders said. \par \par \uc2  A trader with a large U.S. bank said Singapore banks, in particular, were unloading their ringgit positions, reacting to persistent rumors that Malaysia could report a 17-billion-ringgit current account deficit. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 24-03-95 \par \par \uc2  0953GMT \par \par \uc2  1026 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011030dr3o001oc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pic